# A147 — the conversation index is read off the main actor (2026-09-16)

## What the request did
# `APIServer.sharedPage` called `service.store.conversation(id:)` directly. That store keeps every kept
# conversation in one file, `conversations.json` (A137's single atomic replacement), so the lookup reads
# and JSON-decodes *all* of them — up to `ConversationStore.maximumKept`, 200 — and it did so on the
# main actor, which is also where the engine's turn loop runs. A well-formed id nobody had heard of cost
# exactly as much as a known one.

## The fix
# `ConversationStore.listOffMainActor()` and `conversationOffMainActor(id:)` run the decode in a
# `Task.detached`; `APIServer.sharedPage` and the three `EngineService` read sites
# (`.listSavedConversations` twice, `.openSavedConversation`) use them. The storage format is
# deliberately unchanged: one file per conversation is a migration, and this finding is about which
# actor does the work.

## The test, and the first version of it that was wrong
# `AuditS3SharePageIndexTests` holds the read open with a semaphore (`ConversationStore.readIndex` is
# injectable for exactly this) and asks whether a main-actor task can run while it is still open — the
# shape A15 used for document conversion.

$ swift test --filter SharePageIndexTests          # with the fix
  ✔ Test "A share link finds its conversation, and an unknown one gets the page that says so"
  ✔ Test "The Kept list comes through the same off-actor read"
  ✔ Test "The main actor answers while the conversation index is being read"
  ✔ Test run with 3 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.216 seconds.

$ swift test --filter SharePageIndexTests          # with `conversation(id:)` put back in the route
  ✔ Test "The Kept list comes through the same off-actor read"
  ✘ Test "The main actor answers while the conversation index is being read"
      Expectation failed: responsive
      the main actor could not run while the conversation index was being read
  ✘ Test run with 3 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.260 seconds with 1 issue.
  # and it does not hang: the semaphore is released before the assertion, so the old code finishes the
  # request rather than leaving the main actor holding it.

# The first version of that test measured ticks of a main-actor loop during a ~19 MB decode. It passed
# on its own (25 ticks, longest gap 7 ms, 95 ms request) and **failed in the full gate** — ticks 0,
# longest gap ≥ half the request — because the gate runs the instrumented suite where every test shares
# one main actor: it was measuring the suite's contention, not the read. Replaced by the deterministic
# form above, which is why the numbers here are counts of tests rather than milliseconds.

